411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Hearing loss is one of the most common age-related impairments and it has direct links to cognitive decline. Large studies show that hearing impairment is associated with faster declines in memory, attention and cognition \(Lin et al., 2020\). Other research indicates that untreated hearing loss increases the risk of dementia, partially because of reduced communication and social isolation \(Deal et al., 2017\). Since online content increasingly relies on audio and video, designers should ensure clear transcripts and captions to reduce barriers for older users.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Motor Skills)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Cognitive Impairment)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 241 0 R
/E (Normal aging leads to slower processing speed, reduced working memory and decreased attentional capacity. Research shows that older adults experience progressive declines in recall and processing speed \(Salthouse, 1996\). Other studies on aging also show that reductions in working memory and processing speed are strongly tied to cognitive decline over time \(Caspi, Zimprich & Spitzer, 2022\). These changes result in older adults often struggling with multi-step tasks, inconsistent navigation or dense information. This means that interfaces for this target group must be predictable and linear, so that users are not confronted with multiple choices or unexpected steps at the same time.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Mild cognitive impairment related challenges)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
417 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Task performance and cognitive load)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
418 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Individuals with MCI can face significant challenges when using digital interfaces. Research shows that people with MCI take longer to complete tasks, make more mistakes, and struggle more with complex or multi-step processes compared to older adults without cognitive impairments.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
420 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Navigation and orientation)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Eye-tracking studies indicate that they shift their gaze around the screen more frequently, suggesting uncertainty about where to click or what action to take next \(Haesner et al., 2017\). Users may lose track of where they are within a process, forget previous steps, or become disoriented when too many actions or navigation layers are available at once. Interfaces must therefore support orientation, predictability and step-by-step progression.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Research suggests that linear navigation, where users follow a guided sequence of steps, reduces disorientation and cognitive load, making digital tasks more manageable \(Castilla et al., 2018\).)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Authentication and memory load)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 242 0 R
/E (Authentication steps often create friction: remembering passwords, managing two-factor authentication codes, or handling CAPTCHAs can be overwhelming. To support users, the platform should minimize the need for memory recall.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
